ent to homeless so  desperate for money to feed his  addiction he robbed two places landing  him in jail for the first time  yeah it feels like a long time from  where I'm sitting  Global News first met Hudson in jail  once he got out it took just a few days  before he broke his parole conditions  getting drunk and then coming clean  about it with his parole officer was  enough to land him back behind bars  once I had you know disclosed that I had  drank she had no choice that the system  is set up to send me back and the  criminalization of addiction is  something Hudson says needs to change  he feels fortunate to have served about  half of his nearly two years behind bars  in this Brockville Ontario treatment  center but with only a hundred beds he  got more help than many others so I've  been out today's my fifth day in October  Hudson was released trying to rebuild a  life nervous about relapse I'm really  grateful to have a comfortable space to  sleep like I said when I when I crawled  into bed at night I think about the guys  that are sleeping in shelters I woke up  clean and sober this morning I have no  intentions of using today and and  hopefully I go to bed the same way and  and then I and then I rinse and repeat  Hudson was able to stay sober for about  a month but when Global News met with  him last week I've been drinking  sporadically it's not it's not  continuous at this point but it's enough  to be concerning I can't be the person I  was meant to be when I'm drinking and  using he knows recovery doesn't happen  overnight and he knows his story isn't  unique  Hudson hopes sharing it may give others  like him a voice one that so often goes  unheard Abigail Beaman global news  Ottawa those who work with people like  Eliot Hudson say one of the biggest  challenges people I can face is stigma  about their addiction and lack of  understanding about treatment tomorrow  Abigail Beeman looks at how things need  to change that's in part two of our  series addicted and imprisoned next the  unique connection between a Toronto  radio producer and an alleged al-qaeda  explosives expert  imagine discovering someone who shares  exactly the same name as you that's kind  of freaky itself but this next story  takes that coincidence many steps  further a Canadian radio producer not  only discovered his namesake he traced  his story all the way to Guantanamo Bay  the detention facility set up by the US  during the war on terror and as Jeff's  simple reports had turned into a  fascinating three-year journey lot if  Nasser isn't a name you hear every day  in fact for a long time this radio  producer from Toronto thought he was the  only one until three years ago this was  the first time I'd ever found someone of  my name and he happens to be in the most  notorious prison on earth this was the  tweet that just launched me on this  entire investigation Nasser was idly  scrolling through Twitter when he came  across this tweet mentioning his name in  a letter to the President of the United  States and I was like what this doesn't  make any sense I read the letter and I  soon discovered it wasn't about me it  was about a guy who had my name who  happened to be a detainee two for four  at Guantanamo Bay the tweet was from a  human rights group calling for the  release of Abdul Latif Nasser locked up  at Guantanamo Bay detention camp since  2002 according to this leaked US  government document Nasser was accused  of being a top military adviser to Osama  bin Laden my first reaction to that was  like oh my god this guy's a monster and  just had this morbid curiosity I needed  to know everything about him I possibly  could  Nasser spent the next three years  investigating his namesakes story  interviewing the man's lawyer traveling  to Guantanamo and Morocco to meet his  family and the more he learned the more  that monster moniker didn't seem to fit  the more I dug into this guy's life I  found that we started in similar places  he was a nerdy Muslim kid from the  suburbs his lawyer says he's endured  years of torture despite never being  charged with a crime in fact in 2016 US  authorities actually approved Nasser's  release but that was before the election  of President Donald  Trump he's really stuck in in limbo  nobody in the current administration is  going to be willing to sign off on  transferring him entirely for political  reasons this is the other lot if I'm  what if Nasser Nasser is now releasing a  podcast detailing the other lot of story  based on dozens of interviews with  friends family military and intelligence  officials but the one person he couldn't  speak with was the man himself it almost  feels like the only place this guy has  ever gotten a trial is it's in my garage  we're going through this in this  meticulous way that I don't think  anybody else ever has before and and and  that isn't the way this is supposed to  work  just simple global news and that is  global national for this Thursday I'm  Donna Friesen  tonight's here Canada is the view from  mount Seymour in North Vancouver BC we'd  love to see your Canada please email it  to us and thanks for watching hope to  see you here again tomorrow but I  [Music]
 

